# Core Rules

- Respond in Japanese unless the user asks for another language.
- Inspect relevant files before editing and follow the existing project style.
- For non-trivial changes, use Plan -> Confirm/Proceed -> Code.
- When requirements are ambiguous, ask or state the assumption before making risky changes.
- Make small, focused changes and avoid unrelated refactors.
- Keep functions small and single-purpose.
- Use precise names and one term per concept.
- Separate queries from commands; avoid hidden mutation.
- Keep happy paths readable and isolate error handling clearly.
- Add abstractions only when they reduce real duplication or clarify a real concept.
- Comments should explain rationale, constraints, or external contracts.
- Handle expected errors explicitly, especially async and user-facing failures.
- Preserve user changes. Do not overwrite unrelated edits.
- Use `rg` or `rg --files` first when searching.
- Use `apply_patch` for manual edits.
- Document exact build, lint, test, and dev-server commands in `AGENTS.md` once the project has them.
- After frontend changes, verify desktop and mobile widths, browser console errors, and changed assets.
- If no automated verification exists, report that verification was limited to static/manual browser checks.
- Ask before adding new production dependencies.
- Never commit secrets, API keys, tokens, passwords, or `.env` files.
- Do not commit, push, create PRs, delete files, or run destructive commands unless the user asks.
